---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u, SITE_NAME } from "../lib/site";
import { TOOLS } from "../lib/tools";
---
<Base title="Radiation protection calculators"
      description="Seven calculators for routine radiation protection work — dose rate and shielding, decay, detection limits, unit conversion, specific activity, beta shielding and job planning. Free, no sign-in.">
  {/* ★ 머리는 **이름**이고 문장이 아니다(2026-09-14 실측으로 바꿨다).
        전에는 h1 이 「The calculations you actually repeat…」 한 문장이었고, 그 아래
        석 줄짜리 설명이 더 붙어 **첫 도구가 536px 에서 시작했다** — 390px 화면에서
        일곱 중 **둘**만 보였다. 처음 온 사람은 무엇이 있는 곳인지 알기 전에 문장부터
        해석해야 했다. 아래 「Why the constants are computed」 절이 같은 설명을 전문으로
        들고 있으므로 머리에서 덜어내도 내용은 잃지 않는다. */}
  <h1 class="max-w-3xl text-[28px] font-semibold leading-tight tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-[36px]">
    Radiation protection calculators
  </h1>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
    The calculations you actually repeat — with every constant computed from IAEA and NIST primary
    data rather than copied from a handbook table, so you can see what produced the number.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-2 text-[14px] text-ink-faint">
    Free. No sign-in, no account, nothing you type leaves your browser.
  </p>

  <ul class="mt-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
    {TOOLS.map((t) => (
      <li class="card p-4 transition-colors hover:border-accent/50">
        {/* ★ 그림을 **글 옆**에 둔다 — 위에 띠로 얹으면 카드마다 68px 이 붙어
            첫 화면에 서던 도구가 셋에서 둘로 준다(진입장벽 작업을 되돌리는 셈이다).
            옆에 두면 글이 이미 56px 보다 높아 **카드 높이가 늘지 않는다**. */}
        <a href={u(`/${t.slug}/`)} class="flex gap-3 no-underline">
          {/* ★ 112px 이다 — 세선 잉크 그림은 **크기가 있어야 디테일이 읽힌다.** 56px 에서는
              해칭이 뭉개져 회색 덩어리가 된다(실측으로 두 크기를 나란히 놓고 골랐다).
              ★ 그런데도 **카드는 안 커진다** — 가장 짧은 글 블록이 124px 이라 112px 타일이
              그 안에 들어간다. 이보다 키우면 카드가 늘고 첫 화면 도구 수가 준다. */}
          <img src={u(`/img/tools/${t.slug}.webp`)} width="112" height="112" alt="" aria-hidden="true"
               loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               class="h-28 w-28 shrink-0 rounded-lg border border-line" />
          <div class="min-w-0">
            <h2 class="text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink">{t.name}</h2>
            <p class="mt-1 text-[14px] leading-snug text-ink-muted">{t.question}</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-[12px] leading-snug text-ink-faint">{t.blurb}</p>
          </div>
        </a>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <section class="prose-doc mt-16 max-w-3xl text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
    <h2>Why the constants are computed, not copied</h2>
    <p>
      A gamma dose rate constant taken from a table is a single number whose assumptions are
      invisible. You cannot see which photon lines were included, which low-energy cutoff was
      applied, or which evaluation of the decay data it came from — and those choices change the
      answer. For Am-241, moving the cutoff from 20 keV to 10 keV raises the constant roughly
      eightfold.
    </p>
    <p>
      So this site computes the constants instead. Photon energies and emission probabilities come
      from the IAEA Livechart interface to the evaluated nuclear structure data; mass attenuation and
      mass energy-absorption coefficients come from the NIST tables. Summing over the spectrum gives
      the constant directly, and the cutoff becomes a control you can move rather than a hidden
      assumption. The computed values agree with published constants to within about 2% for the
      well-characterised nuclides, and that comparison is part of the test suite.
    </p>

    <h2>What is covered</h2>
    <p>
      147 nuclides, of which 96 emit photons and 45 emit only beta or alpha radiation — the pure beta
      and alpha emitters that dominate waste characterisation and decommissioning work, including
      H-3, C-14, Ni-63, Sr-90, Tc-99, I-129, Zr-93, Se-79 and the transuranics. Eight shielding
      materials with attenuation data from 1 keV to 20 MeV.
    </p>

    <h2>What is deliberately absent</h2>
    <p>
      Skin dose from surface contamination is not calculated, because doing it properly needs a point
      kernel over the source geometry and the answer is very sensitive to inputs a simple form cannot
      capture. Buildup factor coefficients are not shipped, because the standard tabulation is the
      body of a paid consensus standard; the buildup form is implemented and takes coefficients you
      supply. Where a calculation would be misleading without information the tool does not have, it
      says so rather than returning a number.
    </p>
    <p>
      None of this replaces an accredited calibration, a shielding design, a dose assessment or a
      survey. It is for orientation, for cross-checking something you have already calculated, and
      for seeing how an answer moves when an assumption does.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
